{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His early life is not well documented but it is known that he was raised in the County of Tripoli. He succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152. Raymond III was only around 12 years old when he became Count so his mother Hodierna of Jerusalem acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III's early reign was marked by conflicts with the Byzantine Empire and the Fatimid Caliphate. In 1152 he led a campaign against the Fatimid city of Ascalon but was defeated. In 1156 he allied himself with the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos and together they attacked the Fatimid city of Tortosa.\n\n*Marriage and Children*\n\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures in 1174. Eschiva was the daughter of Walter of Bures and Eschiva of Saint-Omer. The marriage was likely arranged to strengthen the alliance between the County of Tripoli and the Principality of Galilee.\n\nRaymond III and Eschiva had two daughters together: Alice and Melisende. Alice married Bohemond IV of Antioch while Melisende married Bohemond IV's son Raymond-Roupen.\n\n*Imprisonment and Later Life*\n\nIn 1174 Raymond III was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din during the Battle of Banias. He was imprisoned for several years but was eventually released in 1176. After his release Raymond III returned to the County of Tripoli and continued to rule until his death in 1187.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his godson Bohemond IV of Antioch. His daughters Alice and Melisende went on to play important roles in the politics of the Crusader states.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0042633567